WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.221
Bonjour ! My name is Sophie, and I live in

2
00:00:04.222 --> 00:00:10.121
France. Today I am visiting my grandfather at his vineyard where he grows grapes for making

3
00:00:10.122 --> 00:00:13.547
wine. Wine is made from grapes and contains

4
00:00:13.548 --> 00:00:16.367
alcohol. I can’t drink wine because I’m too

5
00:00:16.368 --> 00:00:21.104
young. I can tell you how it is made because my grandfather makes

6
00:00:21.105 --> 00:00:27.965
wine! Today adults drink wine with dinner at home or in a restaurant, but wine has a long

7
00:00:27.966 --> 00:00:33.668
history. Archaeologists discovered wine jugs in Iran that were seven thousand years

8
00:00:33.669 --> 00:00:40.993
old. In Ancient Egypt the pharaohs were buried with wine so they could drink wine in the

9
00:00:40.994 --> 00:00:47.335
afterlife. The ancient Greeks and Romans drank wine mixed with water and used wine in religious

10
00:00:47.336 --> 00:00:54.860
ceremonies. When the Roman Empire expanded, the Romans brought wine grapes to France, Italy, Spain, and

11
00:00:54.861 --> 00:01:01.087
Germany. From the fifth century until the Middle Ages, Christian monks were the main winemakers of

12
00:01:01.088 --> 00:01:04.653
Europe. The monks made wine at the monasteries where they

13
00:01:04.654 --> 00:01:09.650
lived. They improved the quality of wine and developed better methods for making

14
00:01:09.651 --> 00:01:13.753
wine. Making wine is not difficult, but you need lots of

15
00:01:13.754 --> 00:01:17.006
patience. It takes at least a year before the wine is

16
00:01:17.007 --> 00:01:20.088
ready! Let me tell you how my grandfather makes

17
00:01:20.089 --> 00:01:25.237
wine. First Grandfather grows the grapes in cool weather with lots of

18
00:01:25.238 --> 00:01:29.801
sun. In France where we live, the weather is perfect for growing

19
00:01:29.802 --> 00:01:35.456
grapes. Grapes grow on vines, and the field where the grapes grow is called a

20
00:01:35.457 --> 00:01:40.069
vineyard. When the grapes are ripe, Grandfather harvests the grapes from the

21
00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:44.344
vines. He takes them to the winery and puts them in a huge

22
00:01:44.345 --> 00:01:48.363
vat. The ripe grapes are juicy and sweet and full of

23
00:01:48.364 --> 00:01:51.592
sugar. The next step is to crush the

24
00:01:51.593 --> 00:01:56.030
grapes. A long time ago, people used their feet to crush the

25
00:01:56.031 --> 00:02:02.204
grapes. They would climb into the vat with bare feet and jump and stomp until the grapes were

26
00:02:02.205 --> 00:02:06.376
crushed. Boy, that sounds like fun, and very messy

27
00:02:06.377 --> 00:02:10.461
too! My grandfather uses a machine to crush the

28
00:02:10.462 --> 00:02:15.932
grapes. Crushing the grapes breaks the skins and turns them into a wet, juicy

29
00:02:15.933 --> 00:02:21.326
pulp. Over the next few days, yeast on the grape skins causes the wine to

30
00:02:21.327 --> 00:02:25.926
ferment. When the wine ferments, the sugar in the grapes changes to

31
00:02:25.927 --> 00:02:30.003
alcohol. The juice and pulp in the vat will begin to fizz.

32
00:02:30.004 --> 00:02:38.696
After a week the fizzing subsides. Now my grandfather must separate the liquid from the skins, pulp, and seeds.

33
00:02:38.697 --> 00:02:44.228
He uses a machine called a winepress, and then he pours the wine into barrels.

34
00:02:44.229 --> 00:02:48.086
Until now, the wine has been open to the air.

35
00:02:48.087 --> 00:02:53.002
But now the barrels are sealed to keep the air away from the wine.

36
00:02:53.003 --> 00:02:57.235
Three weeks later the fermentation is finally complete.

37
00:02:57.236 --> 00:03:02.833
Now the wine is ready for the final step. This step is called racking.

38
00:03:02.834 --> 00:03:10.480
My grandfather filters the leftover yeast, grape seeds, and grape skins from the wine, and scrapes the barrel clean.

39
00:03:10.481 --> 00:03:16.113
Then he pours the wine back into the barrel and seals it airtight.

40
00:03:16.114 --> 00:03:19.805
The barrels of wine are stored in a cool, dark place.

41
00:03:19.806 --> 00:03:24.201
Four months later, Grandfather racks the wine one more time.

42
00:03:24.202 --> 00:03:33.100
Now the wine is perfectly clear. Grandfather puts the barrels away in a cool, dark place again and leaves them for a whole year!

43
00:03:33.101 --> 00:03:37.518
My grandfather keeps his barrels of wine in the cellar.

44
00:03:37.519 --> 00:03:40.369
One year later, the wine is ready!

45
00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:47.704
Sometimes vineyards wait two years or longer before they sell their wine, and some wines are kept for decades.

46
00:03:47.705 --> 00:03:51.029
People say that wine gets better with age.

47
00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:58.023
The wine is poured into bottles, and each bottle is plugged with a cork to prevent air getting in.

48
00:03:58.024 --> 00:04:03.202
Corks are made from the bark of the cork tree, which grows mainly in Portugal.

49
00:04:03.203 --> 00:04:09.613
Cork is very elastic, but it is also airtight. It is ideal for wine bottles.

50
00:04:09.614 --> 00:04:15.351
France is famous for its wine, and many tourists visit France just to tour the wineries.

51
00:04:15.352 --> 00:04:20.303
However, wine grapes are grown in warm climates all over the world.

52
00:04:20.304 --> 00:04:30.420
France and Italy have the oldest wineries, but Australia, New Zealand, Chile, South Africa, and California are all famous for their wine as well.

53
00:04:30.421 --> 00:04:34.492
Now I will help Grandfather get the wine ready for the stores.

54
00:04:34.493 --> 00:04:37.770
First, we put fancy labels on the bottles.

55
00:04:37.771 --> 00:04:40.834
Then, we put the wine bottles into boxes.

56
00:04:40.835 --> 00:04:48.739
Tomorrow a truck will pick up the boxes and take them to restaurants, bars, supermarkets, and wine stores.

57
00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:51.751
Thank you for visiting my grandfather’s vineyard.

58
00:04:51.752 --> 00:04:54.882
Now you know everything about making wine.

59
00:04:54.883 --> 00:04:59.049
Next time you can help us harvest the grapes!

